    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so in the AC circuit, since it's constantly charging and discharging, is there like a specific reason why it doesn't just block the current like it does in DC? like, what's actually happening at the component level that lets the signal pass through even though the plates aren't actually touching?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At the component level, the capacitor's role is to store electrical energy in an electric field between its plates, which are separated by a dielectric insulator. In an AC signal, the voltage polarity is constantly changing, meaning the capacitor is always charging in one direction and then immediately discharging in the opposite direction. This continuous, cyclical charging and discharging allows current to flow back and forth through the circuit, effectively letting the alternating signal \"pass through\" while still storing energy.",
      "source": "target",
