    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i think i get the charging/discharging part. but i'm still a little confused about the phase shift. why is it that the current peaks before the voltage does? like, how does the timing of the current flow get offset from the voltage cycle?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The current peaking before the voltage does is due to the capacitor's inherent time delay while accumulating enough charge to build up the voltage potential. The capacitor resists sudden changes in voltage; therefore, when the voltage is changing most rapidly, the current peaks. Essentially, the current leads the voltage because the capacitor has to \"build up\" the voltage across it by storing charge first.",
